      "problem": "Factor $-9{w}^{2}-16.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's factor $-1$ from the expression. We have\n\n$$\n-9{w}^{2}-16=-(9{w}^{2}+16).\n$$\n\nLet's now focus on factoring the expression $(9{w}^{2}+16).$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{w}^{2}+16 & =9{w}^{2}-(-16) \\\\  & =9{w}^{2}-(16{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3w)^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(3w+4\\text{i})(3w-4\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n-(9{w}^{2}+16)=-(3w+4\\text{i})(3w-4\\text{i}).\n$$",
