      "dom_id": "question-46526",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "${w}^{4}+8{w}^{2}+16=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${w}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{4}+8{w}^{2}+16 & ={({w}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\,({w}^{2})+{4}^{2} \\\\  & ={({w}^{2}+4)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{2}+4 & ={w}^{2}-(-4) \\\\  & ={w}^{2}-(4{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={w}^{2}-{(2\\text{i})}^{2} \\\\  & =(w+2\\text{i})(w-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{4}+8{w}^{2}+16 & ={({w}^{2}+4)}^{2} \\\\  & ={((w+2\\text{i})(w-2\\text{i}))}^{2} \\\\  & =(w+2\\text{i})^{2}(w-2\\text{i})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
